<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>What an estimate review gives you</h2>
<p>Insurance estimates are dense: hundreds of line codes, unit prices, quantities and depreciation percentages. Most policyholders can see the total is too low but can’t see why. An estimate review translates that document into a plain list of what’s missing, what’s mispriced and what’s mis-measured — and how much each issue is worth.</p>
<h2>What we check, line by line</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Price list and date.</strong> Is the estimate priced for your Georgia region and the current month, or an outdated list?</li>
<li><strong>Quantities.</strong> Roof squares, wall square footage, linear feet of trim and flooring checked against our own measurements.</li>
<li><strong>Missing trades</strong> — electrical, plumbing, HVAC, insulation, cleaning, permits — that are needed to complete the work.</li>
<li><strong>Overhead and profit.</strong> Whether a general contractor is reasonably needed to coordinate multiple trades.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> Rates applied to labor, non-depreciable items, and whether holdback can be recovered.</li>
<li><strong>Code items</strong> required by local building departments and covered by ordinance-or-law provisions.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Then you decide the next step</h2>
<p>With a review in hand, you can submit a supplement request to the carrier, share it with your contractor, or — if the gap remains — move to appraisal with a valuation already built. About a third of the reviews we perform close the gap without any formal appraisal at all.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">See the gap first</b>Our <a href="/claim-tools/appraisal-value-estimator/">appraisal value estimator</a> gives a quick read on whether the difference between estimates is large enough to justify appraisal costs.</div>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can you review an estimate before the adjuster finishes?</summary><div class="a"><p>We can review any draft you receive, but reviews are most useful on the carrier’s full estimate after the inspection, when the scope is set.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I need to send the estimate in a particular format?</summary><div class="a"><p>A PDF of the full estimate, including the summary and line items, is ideal. Photos the adjuster took and any reinspection reports help as well.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is an estimate review a commitment to appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. It’s a standalone service. You decide what to do with it.</p></div></details></div>
